#
# iceland-thermal: the stock skin-temperature policy of the OnePlus Pad 4 under mainline Linux.
#
# On stock the kernel's thermal trips are only the backstop. The policy is in userspace: the "horae" service
# estimates the temperature of the shell (front, frame, back) from several board thermistors and the battery, turns
# it into a "temperature gear" (0..19), and the thermal control service applies a table per gear: CPU frequency caps
# per cluster, a GPU cap, a brightness cap, a charging cool-down level. Mainline cannot express a weighted
# multi-sensor zone in the device tree, so this service does the same in userspace. The kernel zones (CPU junction
# 105 / 112 / 114 C, XO 78 / 80 / 90 C, the hot-spot thermistor's own loop) stay as they are, underneath.
#
# Every number is the Pad 4's stock 11.A.38 value (KNOWLEDGE rule 13), unless marked OURS. Sources (files of the
# user's own partition carve, B = ~/mainline/build/fwsuper):
#  gear thresholds   B/x-system_ext/etc/horae/horae.conf (decoded) thermal_status_model, lines 5-7:
#                    temp_threshold 25 27 29 31 33 35 37 39 40 41 43 45 47 49 51 53 55 57 59 61 63 (C, entering),
#                    temp_threshold_down 23 25 ... 61 (leaving), thermal_status -1 0 1 .. 19 (the gear)
#  shell estimate    B/x-odm/etc/horae/horae_target.conf (decoded), project 25978, shell_temp_fitting_model: five
#                    models (front, front_1, frame, back, back_1) over sys-therm-2, sys-therm-4, board_temp, battery
#                    and chg_cp_therm, four coefficient rows each (the present sample and the three before it, one
#                    every 5 s) plus a constant; until four samples exist stock uses the battery temperature plus
#                    bat_bias (2.0 C). The coefficients are NOT in this file: they are read from
#                    /etc/iceland/thermal-model.json (stock's model, decoded, generic: a file of iceland-firmware
#                    >= 11.A.38-3). Without that file the battery fallback is used all the time.
#  CPU levels        B/x-odm/etc/temperature_profile/sys_thermal_control_config.xml:24-37 (thermal_cpulevel:
#                    maxCpuFreq "cluster 0, cluster 1" in kHz; levels 10 and 13 also take the two big cores away,
#                    maxCpuCore "-1,0" - mainline has no CPU pause, the frequency cap alone is applied)
#  GPU levels        same file :40-46 (thermal_gpulevel, maxGpuFreq in Hz)
#  per-gear table    same file :53-72 (globalPolicy: cpu, gpu, brightness, charge per tempGear); every other
#                    section (default, apps, scenes) starts from this table (Battery.apk: the policy of a gear is
#                    the globalPolicy entry of that gear, overridden by the attributes the section names)
#  benchmark table   same file :715-723 (the benchmark app list: no CPU cap up to gear 12, level 12 at 13-14,
#                    level 13 from 15; GPU level 6 from gear 13)
#  mode offsets      same file :10-12 racing_mode_item -4, normal_mode_item 0, powersave_mode_item 1 (added to
#                    the gear), :17 high_temp_safety_level_item 15
#  brightness        B/x-system_ext/etc/display_brightness_config_common.xml:2777-2800 temperature_limit_nit
#                    (level -> nits) and B/x-my_product/vendor/etc/display_brightness_config_P_D.xml (nits -> the
#                    12-bit backlight level, the same scale as the mainline KTZ8869 driver, max 4095):
#                    800 nits = 3744 (:3758), 700 = 3500 (:3514), 600 = 3241 (:3255), 500 = 2963 (:2977),
#                    450 = 2817 (:2831), 400 = 2662 (:2676), 350 = 2498 (:2512), 300 = 2323 (:2337),
#                    250 = 2133 (:2147), 200 = 1925 (:1939), 150 = 1685 (:1699), 100 = 1402 (:1416)
#  charge cool-down  B/dtbo/dt00.dts:4220-4223 cool_down_pdqc_vol_mv 5000 5000 5000 9000 with cool_down_pdqc_curr_ma
#                    1200 1500 2000 1500, cool_down_normal_curr_ma 1200 1500 2000 (level 1, 2, 3, 4)
# OURS (not stock values):
#  - the caps are written to cpufreq's and devfreq's own maximum (scaling_max_freq, max_freq), the smaller of the
#    power mode's value (read from the active tuned profile) and the thermal cap; written again every period,
#    because the kernel offers no way to read the requested maximum back;
#  - GNOME's power modes take stock's mode offsets: Power Saver +1, Balanced 0, Performance -4 (thermal.conf);
#    from gear 15 (stock's high-temperature safety level) a negative offset no longer applies;
#  - the charging cool-down is passed to iceland-battery-guard as a battery-current cap in
#    /run/iceland/thermal-fcc-ma: levels 1-3 as stock's input currents (1200 / 1500 / 2000 mA, never more than
#    stock's 5 V input can deliver), level 4 and above as 3000 mA (stock: 9 V x 1.5 A input = 13.5 W; 13.5 W /
#    4.5 V float voltage = 3.0 A, the least that input can mean for the battery);
#  - a fitted shell temperature outside -30..100 C is ignored; the lag order of the four coefficient rows (first
#    row = newest sample) is read from the row weights, not proven in stock's code (it only changes the estimate
#    while temperatures move);
#  - (0.28-6) brightness: the cap lives in the kernel, as stock's lives in its display HAL. The ktz8869 backlight
#    driver registers a cooling device "ktz8869-backlight" with one state per gear (0..19, max_state 19) and the
#    per-gear limit of the table below built in; the ICs get min(the user's brightness, the gear's limit) while the
#    backlight's "brightness" (what GNOME reads and writes) keeps the user's value. This service writes the
#    effective gear (0 below gear 0) to that device's cur_state, only when it differs, and 0 when it stops or
#    ENABLE / BRIGHTNESS go to 0. No such device (an older kernel) or a different max_state = no brightness cap
#    (one log line). The backlight itself (/sys/class/backlight/*) is never written: in 0.28-3 a sysfs cap fought
#    GNOME 50's own brightness (r150 / r151), 0.28-4 / -5 shipped BRIGHTNESS=0;
#  - (0.23) the battery temperature comes from qcom_battmgr over pmic_glink; while the ADSP stalls its read fails
#    (-110). A battery reading that exists but cannot be read is UNKNOWN, not "absent": the estimate then lacks its
#    battery term (the models and the battery fallback drop out), so for that period the gear may rise but never
#    goes down; it moves freely again once the battery reads. A battery that never existed keeps the old behaviour;
#  - CHARGE_SHIFT=0 (thermal.conf): a negative mode offset (Performance, or a shifted Balanced) does not relax
#    the charging cool-down: the charge cap is taken from the gear itself, as in stock's normal mode. Stock
#    shifts it with the mode (CHARGE_SHIFT=1).
#  - (0.28-8) screen off: X = the stock XML above. Stock has a screenOff section (X:88-98): gear 3 cpu 0; gear 6
#    cpu 7 gpu 3; gear 8 cpu 7 gpu 3; gear 10 cpu 7 gpu 5; gears 11 and 12 cpu 7; gear 14 no CPU / GPU attribute
#    (its other attributes - restrict, charge 0, refreshRate, the dis* switches - have no counterpart here or keep
#    the stricter globalPolicy value: the charging cool-down is NOT relaxed with the screen off, stock's charge="0"
#    would be). Battery.apk (classes2.dex) takes a section's entry for a gear the section does not list by walking
#    DOWN to the nearest listed gear: o9/h.a0() and p9/a.g() call o9/h.w0(.., gear) and, while it returns null and
#    gear >= 0, retry with gear - 1; w0() is an exact SparseArray.get(gear) (w0 pc 504-558 for the "screen_off"
#    key). So gear 7 takes gear 6's entry, 9 takes 8's, 13 takes 12's, 15-19 take 14's, 4-5 take 3's, 0-2 none.
#    (The only TreeMap.ceilingEntry in the APK, classes.dex Lm0/d;->f, is androidx Room's query pool, not the
#    thermal code.) An attribute an entry does not name is -2 (ThermalPolicy.createFromElement pc 84).
#    OURS: with the screen off the CPU and GPU caps are min(globalPolicy, that entry) per cluster in kHz / Hz,
#    never above stock under either reading of a section (override or min); the base table is globalPolicy also in
#    the benchmark mode (stock's screen_off category replaces the app's). "Screen off" = the panel's DSI connector
#    reports dpms "Off" or a backlight reports bl_power != 0 (GNOME's blank does both on this tablet:
#    docs/repo-docs/pc-026-critical-items.md:81-82, DSI-1 dpms=Off, bl_power=4); read once per period and again at
#    every boost start. Neither readable = screen on (as 0.28-7). SCREEN_OFF=0 in thermal.conf switches it off;
#  - (0.28-8) boost: every CPU level has a boostCeiling (X:24-37), the frequency stock's hypnus may reach during a
#    touch / app-launch / scroll boost (Battery.apk sends boost_ceiling, boostBreak1 / boostBreak2 of the gear to
#    hypnus: s9/c$a.d pc 52-132). The boost duration is hypnus's own and is not in the carve (no hypnus binary or
#    config in fwsuper). OURS: on input from a touchscreen, pen, touchpad, mouse or keyboard (evdev, read only, no
#    grab, one epoll, at most one wake-up per 250 ms while input goes on) the CPU maxima go up to min(the mode's
#    maximum, the level's boostCeiling) until BOOST_HOLD_MS (1000) after the last event, then back to the gear's
#    cap. Never: with the screen off, without a CPU cap, at a gear whose boostBreak is -1 (gears 10-19, X:63-72;
#    the meaning of -1 is not in the APK, so the stricter reading, no boost, is taken), while a kernel cpufreq
#    cooling device holds a state (a kernel trip acts), while iceland-battery-guard reports a safety stop
#    (/run/iceland/charge-alert kind=stop, or its input-cut mark), or while the battery reading is unknown. One
#    continuous boost lasts at most boostBreak2 seconds of the gear (X:53-62: 10 / 5 / 3; the unit is an
#    inference), then it waits for BOOST_HOLD_MS without input. The period step stays in charge: it re-checks every
#    gate and ends a boost the gear no longer allows. BOOST=0 in thermal.conf switches it off.
# The kernel's own loop on the skin thermistor (queue head: 48 / 51 C; stock's 60 / 61 C trips are the owner's
# choice) is not touched and cannot be lifted from here: this service writes only user space's maxima
# (cpufreq scaling_max_freq and devfreq max_freq, one frequency QoS request each, never above the power mode's
# value), the kernel's cooling devices hold their own requests and the lowest request wins. It never writes a
# thermal zone or a trip, and of the cooling devices only the backlight's own brightness limit (ktz8869-backlight,
# which no thermal zone binds). So it only ever adds lower limits and takes its own back.
